// =====================================================================
//  RAW — the CFBD game and drive feeds, both seasons, from Hugging Face
//  (huggingface.co/datasets/24601p/cfb-data)
//
//  The model reads two DuckDB TABLES, cfb_games and cfb_drives, not the
//  parquet files. The tables are created when the connection opens, from
//  the setupSQL in malloy-config.json: one read_parquet over every season's
//  file at Hugging Face, stacked.
//
//    * locally (malloyyo dashboard dev / lint, build-site.mjs) the native
//      DuckDB connection runs that setupSQL itself;
//    * on the published site, spa/shell.ts downloads the same files, then
//      runs the same setupSQL in DuckDB-WASM (build-site.mjs hands it over).
//
//  WHY TABLES. Decoding parquet costs about 0.4 s per scan in DuckDB-WASM,
//  even with the file already in memory, and the drive model scans its
//  data several times per query. A scan of the loaded table takes a few
//  milliseconds. The data is still read fresh from Hugging Face on every
//  page load and every new connection, so each pipeline upload
//  (cfbd_pipeline.py --upload) shows up without a rebuild.
//
//  Add a season by adding its URLs to the setupSQL. Game ids and drive ids
//  are unique across seasons (checked: no overlap between 2025 and 2026),
//  so the window functions downstream, which partition by GameId, never
//  mix two seasons.
// =====================================================================

#(doc) Every game of every season in the feed, one row per game, exactly as CFBD publishes it.
source: games_raw is duckdb.table('cfb_games')

#(doc) Every drive of every season in the feed, one row per possession, exactly as CFBD publishes it.
source: drives_raw is duckdb.table('cfb_drives')

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//  TEAM-GAMES — each game twice, once from each team's side.
//
//  A ranking is asked per team, but a game row names two teams in two
//  differently named columns. Unpivoting here gives one row per team per
//  game, so "points allowed" is just points_against and a team's record
//  is a count over its own rows — no pick-the-right-column logic in every
//  measure. Malloy has no UNION, which is why this one lives in SQL.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

#(doc) One row per team per game: every game appears twice, once from the home team's side and once from the away team's.
source: team_games_raw is duckdb.sql("""
  WITH sides AS (
    SELECT Id AS game_id, Season AS season, "Week" AS week_number, SeasonType AS season_type,
      StartDate AS start_date, Completed AS completed, ConferenceGame AS conference_game,
      TRUE AS is_home, HomeTeam AS team, HomeConference AS conference, HomeClassification AS classification,
      AwayTeam AS opponent, AwayConference AS opponent_conference, AwayClassification AS opponent_classification,
      HomePoints AS points_for, AwayPoints AS points_against,
      HomePregameElo AS pregame_elo, HomePostgameElo AS postgame_elo, AwayPregameElo AS opponent_pregame_elo
    FROM cfb_games
    UNION ALL
    SELECT Id, Season, "Week", SeasonType,
      StartDate, Completed, ConferenceGame,
      FALSE, AwayTeam, AwayConference, AwayClassification,
      HomeTeam, HomeConference, HomeClassification,
      AwayPoints, HomePoints,
      AwayPregameElo, AwayPostgameElo, HomePregameElo
    FROM cfb_games
  )
  SELECT *,
    CAST(game_id AS VARCHAR) || '|' || team AS team_game_id,
    -- the team's most recent game that carries a postgame Elo, so a
    -- ranking can show where the rating stands now
    postgame_elo IS NOT NULL
      AND row_number() OVER (
        PARTITION BY team, season, postgame_elo IS NULL ORDER BY start_date DESC
      ) = 1 AS is_latest_rated_game
  FROM sides
""")
